2026-09-08T08:02Z claude note Found by following the author's live journal on doudou during the 2026-09-08 pre-release review, not by reading code. The census reported 406 "could not be indexed", which is what sent me looking; the number turned out to conflate two unrelated facts, and the residue under it is this defect. Author's rulings, same session, verbatim in substance: (1) "ne pas croire l'etiquette, le B A BA du consommateur averti"; (2) "oui on persiste en cache, jetable c'est un cache"; (3) the census categories are to be separated in the dialog's account, which is filed against 0693 rather than here.
2026-09-08T10:14Z claude note Both actions implemented on t0740-magic-number-and-persisted-refusal. Action 1: an eight-byte magic-number read in inspect(), gated on the queued class so it costs one read per candidate and nothing at all on an indexed library; a mismatch is `unsupported`. Action 2: a new `host.refuse` writes the "no current pack" verdict into the disposable cache against the identity, read back BEFORE the pack branch (which would otherwise drop the record), surfacing as a new `failed-remembered` status kept inside the failed class so the "could not be indexed" figure does not shrink. Both tests confirmed red against the unfixed plugin with the new mock in place, under a collecting harness so the two reds are the only ones. Mutants 33/33 and 15/15 caught. The live-library census sum is the one verification line this machine cannot close: no synced library and no SDT packs here.
2026-09-08T10:14Z claude note GOVERNANCE, caught by the pre-PR adherence gate and worth recording because the code was finished before anyone noticed. Persisting a refusal contradicted four standing sites — SPEC.md, the 2026-09-05 RATIFIED entry, the shipped manifest description and verification/SDT-SITTER-LAUNCH.md — and the author's superseding ruling existed only in this ticket's log. AGENTS.md puts rulings in DECISIONS.md first and SPEC.md second, so the ruling is now appended there, narrowing the 2026-09-05 clause rather than withdrawing it: active jobs stay unpersisted unconditionally, and what is allowed is one identity-keyed verdict in a disposable cache. Manifest version 0.3.3 -> 0.3.6; 0.3.4 and 0.3.5 are skipped because ticket 0727 hand-installed both into the author's Zotero this morning without either reaching git, so the version gate cannot see them.
2026-09-08T11:06Z claude note CORRECTION to the entry above, from round 1 of the review panel: the sweep of standing sites found four and there were FIVE. `createSDTCache`'s own header comment in scheduler.js — "without paths, text, failures or active jobs" — sat directly above the function this change extends with `refuse()`, and a grep for "failure ledger" could not see it because it does not use the phrase. The lesson is the grep, not the count: a sweep keyed on one wording cannot find a site that words the same claim differently. Also fixed in the same round: a paragraph break landed one sentence early in SPEC.md and left the cache paragraph with no privacy guarantee, and SPEC.md's header date was not bumped for a substantive edit. Version 0.3.6 -> 0.3.7 for the payload comment change.

2026-09-08T11:30Z claude note DESIGN CHANGED MID-FLIGHT, by the author, and action 2 is rebuilt rather than repaired. Asked to choose between keeping the persisted verdict with a UI to manage it, dropping suppression entirely, or holding it in memory for the session only, he ruled: session-scoped, gone on restart, nothing written to disk for this purpose, accepting by name that a failure is retried after every Zotero restart in exchange for no orphaned or undiscoverable disk state. That also closes what two review rounds (10 reviewer passes) reproduced independently: a persisted refusal short-circuits inspect() before the pack is ever stat'ed, so a document whose pack later arrives by any route other than this plugin's own ensure() is reported unrecoverable forever, with no UI to clear one entry. And it restores this repo's own verified native contract (verification/SDT-PLUGIN-PREREQUISITES.md:51,95-97): native SDT retries a generic failure on every new call, so a persisted verdict overrode that contract rather than extending it. Implementation: host.refuse, cache.refuse/refused, the persisted `refused` field and the `failed-remembered` status are all removed; the suppression is the scheduler's pre-existing in-memory `failed` Set, which was already session-scoped and already kept a failure out of the candidate list within the activation. Action 1 is untouched — it was never persisted.
2026-09-08T11:30Z claude note GOVERNANCE reversal, appended not amended. The 2026-09-08 DECISIONS.md entry that narrowed the 2026-09-05 "disposable, never a durable failure ledger" ruling is withdrawn by a new entry rather than edited in place; SPEC.md, the manifest description and verification/SDT-SITTER-LAUNCH.md revert to the un-narrowed claim, with one sentence added to each saying that suppression is session-scoped. Confirmed rather than assumed: bootstrap.js's `launch-disable` string ("Errors stay confined to the session... it holds no text and no running job") is true again under this design and needed no edit — a grep across plugins/, verification/ and SPEC.md finds that claim in no other site. Version 0.3.7 -> 0.3.8 in manifest.json and update.json.

2026-09-08T11:46Z claude note Round 3 self-gate. /verify-adherence PASS on the rebased tip. Two round-2 non-blockers fixed here rather than deferred, both being one line: SDT_MAGIC_BYTES is now derived from the table's longest prefix instead of a hardcoded 8 -- a longer signature added later would have skipped its own entry silently, since `prefix.length <= head.length` just goes false and the label stands -- and the sniff's cost comment no longer claims "once each", which was false for exactly the 39 documents the check exists for: only `current` reaches cache.remember(), so a ruled-out document has no record and is re-read every sweep. Left as it stands, with the reasoning: eight bytes per sweep against a native attempt of 50-90 ms is not worth a second store to keep consistent. Every other round-2 finding is closed by the redesign itself (the two refused() logic gaps, the hydration exclusivity gap, the `refuse` event-kind collision, the uncovered host.refuse call site) or was already fixed in round 1 (the SPEC.md antecedent, the SPEC.md date). Version 0.3.8 -> 0.3.9 for this payload change; the sitter-version gate caught that 0.3.8 had already been pushed.

--- body ---
## Context

The sitter's census reports a single figure, "Could not be indexed (last
census): 406". Read on the author's live library on 2026-09-08 it decomposes,
and the classes sum exactly to the scanned total:

    current           13 699
    unsupported        2 600
    missing-source       367
    failed-session        39
    excluded               1
                      ------
    scanned           16 706

So **367 of the 406 are `missing-source`** — the file is not on this disk,
which is an ordinary fact about a synced library and not a failure of anything.
Only **39 are real failures**, and they are what this ticket is about. The
conflation is not cosmetic: it sent this session's own analysis the wrong way
twice before the breakdown was read, which is the argument for the separated
account filed against 0693.

## What the 39 are

The live journal (`bench/results/sdt-sitter-2026-09-08/0.3.5-all-admissions-fail.json`)
names twelve of them by cache key. Resolved against `~/data/Zotero/storage` and
matched by byte size to the `admit` records — every size matches to the digit,
so the mapping is measured and not assumed:

- **Eleven are JPEG images**, page scans of historical works: `Cantor1896-p161
  .jpg`, `-p162`, `-p163`, `Westergaard1890-p220.jpg`, `Sincov1904-p216.jpg`,
  `-p217`, `Frisch1930-p398.jpg`, `-p401`, `-p403`, `Frisch1936-p7.jpg`,
  `Frege1874-p5.jpg`. `file(1)` confirms real `JPEG image data, JFIF standard
  1.01` bytes.
- **One is a genuine HTML snapshot**, `Shananin2009-sciup-snapshot.html`, real
  UTF-8 HTML. Its failure has a DIFFERENT and still unknown cause and must not
  be folded into the image story.

Every failing admission carries `pages: null`, and each takes 50–90 ms from
`admit` to `settle` — far too fast to be extraction trying and losing. The error
is the plugin's own sentence, not a platform exception: `Native SDT did not
persist a current pack`.

## Why an image reaches a text extractor

`inspect()` (bootstrap.js:1259) classifies by the host's own predicates:

    const processor = item.isPDFAttachment() ? 'pdf'
      : item.isEPUBAttachment() ? 'epub'
      : item.isSnapshotAttachment() ? 'snapshot' : null;
    if (!processor) return { status: 'unsupported' };

Read in the installed build (`chrome/content/zotero/xpcom/data/item.js`),
`isSnapshotAttachment()` is

    this.attachmentLinkMode == LINK_MODE_IMPORTED_URL &&
    this.attachmentContentType == 'text/html'

which a JPEG cannot satisfy. Since these items WERE admitted, and `snapshot` is
the only class they could have entered under, **Zotero records these JPEGs with
`attachmentContentType == 'text/html'`**. That step is a deduction from the code
rather than a direct read — `zotero.sqlite` is locked while Zotero runs — but
the code leaves no other path, and it is consistent with how the files were
acquired: a captured page that served an image.

So the plugin trusts a label that is wrong about the bytes underneath it. Native
SDT is handed a document declared HTML whose content is a photograph, finds no
text, persists no pack, and returns. The platform is behaving correctly
throughout — 4 729 of this library's 4 890 snapshot directories DO carry a pack,
so snapshots as a class are handled fine.

## Why it repeats forever

`failed-session` is exactly what its name says: the exclusion lasts the session.
Next activation, the same 39 documents are candidates again, are admitted again,
fail again in the same 50–90 ms, and are counted again. They can never succeed,
and nothing remembers that.

## What this does to ticket 0606

0606 turns on how many documents native extraction genuinely cannot handle,
since that is the case for producing packs ourselves. On the evidence here the
answer is smaller than any figure so far quoted: eleven of twelve sampled
failures are images that a text extractor is right to refuse. They argue for not
submitting them, not for building a producer. Ticket 0728 carries the residue
recount that reaches the same place from the other direction.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `inspect()` at :1254, the processor
  classification at :1259
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — `failed-session` and the candidate queue
- `bench/results/sdt-sitter-2026-09-08/` — the two live journals this rests on
- `/opt/zotero7/app/omni.ja`, `chrome/content/zotero/xpcom/data/item.js` — the
  three `is*Attachment()` predicates as the running build defines them

## Actions

1. **Do not trust the declared type.** Before admitting, confirm the bytes are
   what the label claims — a magic-number read of the first few bytes is enough
   to separate a JPEG from HTML and costs one short read per candidate, once,
   against a document that is otherwise about to be handed to a worker. A
   mismatch is `unsupported`, not a failure: the document is fine, our
   classification of it was wrong.
2. **Hold the refusal for the session, in memory.** Superseded by the author's
   ruling of 2026-09-08T11:30Z (see the log): the cache is session-scoped and
   gone on restart, and nothing is written to disk for this purpose. Within one
   activation a document that failed is not resubmitted; after a restart it is
   asked again, which is the accepted cost and is also what the native service
   does with a generic failure. The mechanism is the scheduler's existing
   in-memory `failed` Set, so what this action really required was measuring
   that the mechanism was already right and stopping the persistence work that
   had been built on top of it. The earlier form of this action — a verdict
   persisted in `sdt-sitter-cache.jsonl` against the identity — is recorded here
   only because the reversal is the interesting part.
3. Keep the genuine HTML snapshot out of this fix and out of its story. One
   real snapshot failing is a separate question; record it and leave it.

## Test

- Red first, for action 1: an attachment whose declared type is `text/html` and
  whose bytes are a JPEG must be classified `unsupported`, and must never reach
  `submit`. Against the current tree it is admitted and fails, so the test
  reddens today. The mock already carries the seams for a fixture of this shape.
- For action 2, the assertion is now two-sided and the second half is the
  redesign's accepted cost stated as a test. Within one activation: drive a
  second sweep through the timer the sitter armed for itself and assert the
  failing document is not resubmitted, asserting on the whole `ensure` call list
  rather than a count of new calls, since a sitter that stopped sweeping
  altogether also resubmits nothing. Across a restart: a fresh activation over
  the same cache file DOES ask the document again. And on the bytes rather than
  on behaviour, since the two would look identical if a verdict were written and
  then ignored: the cache file contains no refusal record at all.
- The mixed-population trap is the one to watch on any count assertion here: a
  test whose fixture holds only failures cannot see a fix that also stops
  admitting the good documents.

## Verification

- [x] A byte/label mismatch is classified `unsupported` and never submitted
- [x] A "no current pack" outcome is not retried within the session, IS retried
      after a restart, and nothing about it is written to the cache file
- [x] Both tests reddened against the unfixed tree before the fix
- [ ] The census total still equals the sum of its classes on the live library
      — not closeable from the machine this was written on, which has no synced
      library and no SDT packs. Both tests assert the sum over their own fixture
      populations; the live reading is the author's to take.
- [x] `make check` passes — every gate green and 1 279 tests pass. One test
      fails on this machine and on `origin/main` alike, verified against a
      detached worktree at the base: `test_identity_switch_outside_the_namespace_runs_where_the_reverse_is_refused`,
      on a rootless-podman storage mismatch that has nothing to do with the
      sitter. Pre-existing and environmental, not this branch's.

## Invariants

- The plugin still never writes to `storage/`, never changes extraction
  settings, and never replaces a source attachment.
- A document is never permanently written off. Nothing about a failure outlives
  the activation that saw it, so no restart inherits a verdict and no state on
  disk can survive the thing it describes.
- Native SDT is not second-guessed. This ticket stops us handing it work it
  cannot do; it does not add extraction of our own.

## Exit criteria

Images declared as HTML are classified rather than submitted, a document that
cannot yield a pack is asked once per activation rather than once per sweep, no
failure verdict reaches the disk, and all of it is held by tests with a
demonstrated red.
